D. Correct. Referral is appropriate when communication barriers cannot be adequately addressed and the clinician cannot provide safe, effective, and competent treatment. The therapist has attempted appropriate support, but the barrier continues to affect assessment and client safety. The transition should be discussed respectfully, suitable options should be provided, and continuity of care should be protected. The referral is based on the inability to meet the client’s clinical and communication needs—not merely on difference or discomfort. Memory cue: Use the referral sequence: Identify the barrier. Attempt appropriate support or adaptation. Assess whether care is now effective and safe. Refer when the need still cannot be met. Protect continuity of care.

